WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.620
Semaphorins guide growing axons by repelling their growth cones from places they

2
00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:13.800
must not enter. Keeping axons out of the wrong territory helps them reach the right

3
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.560
place. In the spinal cord, Sema3A helps keep pain fibres out of the ventral horn,

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.640
while muscle-stretch fibres enter.

5
00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.440
Other cues probably share this work.

6
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.920
A growth cone is the spread fan at an axon tip.

7
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.860
In a dish, a repellent can fold that fan into a thin tip.

8
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.440
That is collapse.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:36.810
How does the growth cone hear Sema3A?

10
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.490
This secreted semaphorin binds neuropilin tightly.

11
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:45.890
But neuropilin has only a short tail inside the cell, with nothing that looks like

12
00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:50.450
an enzyme. A receptor that catches a signal must also pass it on.

13
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.330
Where is the wire?

14
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:54.543
The answer is plexin.

15
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:59.703
In engineered cells, neither protein alone let Sema3A shrink the cell.

16
00:01:00.623 --> 00:01:01.623
Together, they did.

17
00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:05.763
Most class 3 semaphorins reach plexins through neuropilin.

18
00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:09.403
Membrane semaphorins can bind plexins directly.

19
00:01:10.543 --> 00:01:11.543
Neuropilin catches.

20
00:01:12.423 --> 00:01:13.423
Plexin signals.

21
00:01:14.117 --> 00:01:15.617
Other work split the jobs.

22
00:01:16.477 --> 00:01:19.117
A neuropilin without its tail still supported collapse.

23
00:01:20.117 --> 00:01:25.017
A plexin stripped of its inner part blocked Sema3A responses in sensory neurons.

24
00:01:25.897 --> 00:01:26.997
So neuropilin catches.

25
00:01:28.137 --> 00:01:29.137
Plexin signals.

26
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.170
Inside the cell, plexin has a domain that acts as a GAP.

27
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:38.490
It speeds GTP hydrolysis and switches a small GTPase off.

28
00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.030
This can lower integrin adhesion.

29
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:48.150
Plexin signalling also engages Rho family GTPases and proteins that act on actin and

30
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.150
microtubules.

31
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:53.153
The net result is local loss of actin and adhesion.

32
00:01:54.053 --> 00:01:56.473
On one side of a growth cone, that means a turn.

33
00:01:57.373 --> 00:01:58.953
Everywhere at once, it means collapse.

34
00:01:59.913 --> 00:02:02.813
This connects the receptor signal to a change in direction.

35
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:06.697
The response also depends on the receiving cell.

36
00:02:07.577 --> 00:02:13.377
In the developing cortex, Sema3A repels a pyramidal neuron's axon but attracts its

37
00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:17.117
apical dendrite. The same cue can give different answers.

38
00:02:18.077 --> 00:02:23.037
Keep the purpose in view: semaphorin signals help growing axons avoid the wrong

39
00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:25.197
territories and build the right pathways.
